        explain: "Authentication uses system-user access tokens with WhatsApp permissions, stored as secrets and rotated; never embed them in apps. Phone numbers have display names that Meta reviews, and quality ratings that affect limits (lesson 4). Choose between direct Cloud API integration and a BSP based on volume, need for a ready-made inbox, compliance features, and regional support. Pricing changes are frequent (rates update periodically, and regional rules exist, such as restrictions reported on marketing messages to some countries), so put cost estimates in configuration, track spend per template, and review Meta's pricing page each quarter. Throughput: high-volume sends need queueing with rate control and handling of throttling errors; spread campaigns over time. Respect user preferences: WhatsApp users can block or report businesses, which damages quality ratings quickly.",
        example: ['js', 'is a WhatsApp message free, charged, or not allowed?', "function messageCharge(kind, inWindow) {        // kind: service | utility | marketing | authentication\n  if (kind === 'service') return inWindow ? 'free' : 'not_allowed';   // free-form only inside the window\n  if (kind === 'utility') return inWindow ? 'free' : 'charged';\n  if (kind === 'marketing' || kind === 'authentication') return 'charged';\n  return 'unknown';\n}"],
